1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,984
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,984 --> 00:00:04,464
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:04,464 --> 00:00:05,952
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:12,540
PROFESSOR: So next, we will--

5
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:15,660
so we're switching away
from metric spaces,

6
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:18,660
and we talk about
differentiation.

7
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:30,230
So if we have a function
on R, say, and then

8
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:51,860
we say that x that f is
differentiable at x0,

9
00:00:51,860 --> 00:01:11,130
if the limit here f of dx minus
f of x0, divided by x minus x0,

10
00:01:11,130 --> 00:01:16,440
if this limit here
exists as x goes to x0.

11
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:25,310
This quotient here is usually
called the difference quotient.

12
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:35,460
And it's always, of course,
when you form this difference

13
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:38,820
quotient, it's always
assumed, obviously,

14
00:01:38,820 --> 00:01:43,770
that x is not equal to x0 so
that the denominator makes

15
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:44,770
sense.

16
00:01:44,770 --> 00:01:49,440
So if this limit here, as x
goes to x0, if this exists,

17
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:53,700
then we say that the function
f is differentiable at x0.

18
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:59,170
And the limit is called
the derivative of f at x0.

19
00:01:59,170 --> 00:02:03,070
And this is how it's denoted.

20
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:12,880
So now the simplest
possible example of this

21
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:17,410
would be that suppose
that you're looking

22
00:02:17,410 --> 00:02:19,940
at a function that is constant.

23
00:02:19,940 --> 00:02:32,620
So f is equal to f
for some constant C.

24
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:35,720
Then if you're looking at
this difference quotient,

25
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:45,160
so f of dx minus f of x0 x minus
x0, well, the value at any point

26
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:46,010
is C--

27
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:52,510
so this is C minus C
divided by x minus y, x0--

28
00:02:52,510 --> 00:02:56,370
and so the denominator is
0, so this whole thing is 0.

29
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:58,350
And so of course,
the limit-- this

30
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:02,350
is 0 for each x
and also each x0.

31
00:03:02,350 --> 00:03:05,470
So in particular, of course,
the limit here exists.

32
00:03:05,470 --> 00:03:09,300
So the limit here of
the difference quotient

33
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:20,340
exists, and it's equal
to 0 because it's

34
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:24,900
0 for all x for a given x0.

35
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:28,600
So that would be the
simplest possible example.

36
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:32,250
The second simplest example.

37
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:35,430
Second most simple example
would be that you're

38
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:37,060
looking at a linear function.

39
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:42,690
So let's say that f
of x is equal to x.

40
00:03:42,690 --> 00:03:47,765
In this case, the
difference quotient here--

41
00:03:52,830 --> 00:03:55,810
well, this here is x.

42
00:03:55,810 --> 00:03:57,343
This here is x0.

43
00:04:00,490 --> 00:04:03,800
So this here is
for each x and x0.

44
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:05,030
This is 1.

45
00:04:05,030 --> 00:04:08,025
So the limit of course,
in this case, also exists.

46
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,595
The limit here exists,
and it's equal to 1.

47
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:35,140
And so in this case, f is
differentiable at all points.

48
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:39,500
And the derivative at
all points is equal to 0.

49
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:43,280
In this case, f here
is also differentiable.

50
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:52,660
And the derivative at
all points is equal to 1.

51
00:04:55,640 --> 00:04:59,690
So now, in general, how
do we compute the-- so

52
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:02,335
how do we compute the
derivative of a function?

53
00:05:10,490 --> 00:05:17,580
How do we compute
the derivative?

54
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:30,530
And for that, there
are some basic laws--

55
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:31,830
how to compute it.

56
00:05:31,830 --> 00:05:33,280
So there's these laws--

57
00:05:38,270 --> 00:05:45,860
the sum rule, what's
usually called

58
00:05:45,860 --> 00:06:02,040
the Leibniz rule, the
quotient rule, and then

59
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:03,395
also the chain rule.

60
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:17,880
Now let me look at the
first three rules first.

61
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:26,640
So I have two
functions, f and g.

62
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:29,220
They are defined,
say, on R, but it

63
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:35,130
could have been a subset
of R. And I'm assuming

64
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:39,975
that they are both and both--

65
00:06:42,760 --> 00:06:44,320
actually, we'll need one.

66
00:06:44,320 --> 00:07:02,410
So both f and g are
differentiable at x0.

67
00:07:02,410 --> 00:07:04,670
Then we have the following.

68
00:07:04,670 --> 00:07:05,985
So we have the following lemma.

69
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:28,250
One is we have that--

70
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:30,600
so 1, and this is
really the sum rule.

71
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:39,520
We have that the
sum here f plus g--

72
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,400
so the sum of these
two functions, this

73
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:45,710
is the function
that is just in x.

74
00:07:45,710 --> 00:07:50,600
It's just the sum of f
of x and g of x We have

75
00:07:50,600 --> 00:08:04,670
that this function here, f plus
g, is differentiable at x0.

76
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:11,900
And the derivative, f plus g,
the derivative of this at x0

77
00:08:11,900 --> 00:08:14,160
is just the sum
of the derivative.

78
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:31,615
Let me prove this
one here, by the way.

79
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:36,699
So let's prove this.

80
00:08:42,169 --> 00:08:44,130
So we have the sum--

81
00:08:44,130 --> 00:08:49,690
so we are going to look at--
so we formed the difference

82
00:08:49,690 --> 00:09:13,070
quotient, so f plus g at x
minus f plus g at x0 x minus x0.

83
00:09:13,070 --> 00:09:14,780
So now what is this?

84
00:09:14,780 --> 00:09:27,040
So this is just f of x plus
g of x minus and then f of x0

85
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:35,380
plus g of x0, and then
divided by x minus x0.

86
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:38,950
And so then you can
split this up into two.

87
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:41,680
You can write this
difference quotient

88
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:44,240
as if you split it
up if you collect

89
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:47,390
the f's in the numerator.

90
00:09:47,390 --> 00:09:49,330
So I just collect the f's.

91
00:09:53,180 --> 00:09:57,290
And then, afterwards, I collect
and I put it on two different

92
00:09:57,290 --> 00:10:02,530
fractions, separate them out,
and then I collect the g's.

93
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:18,120
And now you see that because
f is differentiable at x0,

94
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:23,730
this thing here, as x goes to
x0, this here goes to f prime.

95
00:10:23,730 --> 00:10:28,380
So this here goes
to f prime of x0.

96
00:10:28,380 --> 00:10:30,870
This is what this
difference quotient here.

97
00:10:30,870 --> 00:10:34,970
And this difference quotient
goes to g prime of 0.

98
00:10:34,970 --> 00:10:38,400
So you have that indeed,
it does converge.

99
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,980
And this is what
it converges to.

100
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:44,370
And so this proves that the
sum of these two functions

101
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:47,280
is also differentiable,
and their derivative

102
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:49,000
is the sum of the derivative.

103
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,480
The next thing we will
prove is Leibniz rule.

104
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:59,460
But before that, I need to
step back and I want to--

105
00:10:59,460 --> 00:11:03,240
so maybe I'll just state
it and then we'll go back

106
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,780
and we need something.

107
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:06,700
The next is what's
called Leibniz rule.

108
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:14,140
So you could also call
it a product rule,

109
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:18,360
but it's really quite generally
called the Leibniz rule.

110
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:21,150
And so this is
when you're looking

111
00:11:21,150 --> 00:11:23,200
at the products of
these functions.

112
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:28,500
So the product
function is a function

113
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:33,785
in x that is just the
product of f of x and g of x.

114
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:52,320
And Leibniz rule
says the following,

115
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:56,500
that if f and g are
differentiable at x0,

116
00:11:56,500 --> 00:11:58,750
then the product of
these two function

117
00:11:58,750 --> 00:12:04,060
here is also
differentiable at x0,

118
00:12:04,060 --> 00:12:06,760
and the derivative
of the product

119
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:11,650
is the derivative of the
first function at x0 times

120
00:12:11,650 --> 00:12:14,920
the value of the
second function at x0

121
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:20,600
plus the symmetric
thing in f and g,

122
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:28,490
which is the value of f at x0
times the derivative of g at x0.

123
00:12:28,490 --> 00:12:31,730
So the product is
differentiable at x0 also.

124
00:12:31,730 --> 00:12:33,950
And this is what
the derivative is.

125
00:12:33,950 --> 00:12:36,970
And in the notes, I just
copied a little bit of thing

126
00:12:36,970 --> 00:12:40,040
just for fun from
Wikipedia about Leibniz

127
00:12:40,040 --> 00:12:41,930
that was kind of an
interesting figure

128
00:12:41,930 --> 00:12:46,920
and played an important
role a long time ago.

129
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:50,840
So before I want to prove
this, let me step back

130
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:59,570
and let me make an observation
about what it means to be

131
00:12:59,570 --> 00:13:01,570
differentiable at a point.

132
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,090
So this is the next lemma.

133
00:13:12,560 --> 00:13:25,530
So you have a function f on R
into R. And you assume that f--

134
00:13:25,530 --> 00:13:38,920
so if f here is
differentiable at x0,

135
00:13:38,920 --> 00:13:52,900
then I claim that then f here
is also continuous at x0.

136
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:58,660
So in other words,
being differentiable

137
00:13:58,660 --> 00:14:01,630
is stronger than
being continuous.

138
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:04,090
In a minute, we will see
an example of something

139
00:14:04,090 --> 00:14:07,210
that's continuous but
not differentiable.

140
00:14:07,210 --> 00:14:12,435
And so now let's
try to prove that.

141
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:27,770
So I'm assuming that
it's differentiable.

142
00:14:27,770 --> 00:14:37,130
So this means that this
difference quotient here

143
00:14:37,130 --> 00:14:38,220
has a limit.

144
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:44,720
And that limit is the
derivative as x goes to x0.

145
00:14:44,720 --> 00:14:59,750
So this means that there exists
a delta greater than 0 such

146
00:14:59,750 --> 00:15:11,930
that if x here minus x0
is less than delta y--

147
00:15:11,930 --> 00:15:17,360
of course, I'm assuming
that it's not equal to 0.

148
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:22,910
If it is less than, the
difference between x and x0

149
00:15:22,910 --> 00:15:26,150
is less than delta,
then this difference

150
00:15:26,150 --> 00:15:36,060
quotient here, then f of
dx minus f of x here x

151
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:42,150
minus x minus x0
minus f prime of x0--

152
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:47,050
this thing here have
to be less than 1.

153
00:15:47,050 --> 00:15:50,700
I mean, this is just using
that this limit exists,

154
00:15:50,700 --> 00:15:52,620
and I'm using--

155
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:55,145
in place of epsilon I'm using 1.

156
00:16:06,330 --> 00:16:13,050
Now so this means just
multiplying over and multiplying

157
00:16:13,050 --> 00:16:13,840
over.

158
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:21,600
You get that f of x
minus f of x minus 0

159
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:30,675
minus f prime of x0,
this thing here--

160
00:16:33,670 --> 00:16:47,530
and I'm multiplying both
sides, so multiply both sides

161
00:16:47,530 --> 00:16:51,890
by x minus x0.

162
00:16:51,890 --> 00:16:53,800
This is a positive thing.

163
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,540
So multiplying both
things by that,

164
00:16:56,540 --> 00:17:01,540
so then I get this
thing here, x minus x0.

165
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:06,970
And then I get less or less
strictly less than x minus x0

166
00:17:06,970 --> 00:17:09,640
because this is
strictly positive

167
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:11,770
because x is not equal to x0.

168
00:17:11,770 --> 00:17:13,480
So I have that.

169
00:17:13,480 --> 00:17:18,530
And so I get that-- so
now I just split it up

170
00:17:18,530 --> 00:17:30,620
so I have that f of dx minus
f of x0 is now less than x

171
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:41,810
minus x0 plus f prime of x
minus 0 times x minus x0.

172
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:47,810
I mean, this is just
the triangle inequality

173
00:17:47,810 --> 00:17:54,920
that we've used many
times, just depend on R.

174
00:17:54,920 --> 00:18:00,080
And so now you see
that if you now--

175
00:18:05,810 --> 00:18:09,970
and this here, of course,
I can also write like this.

176
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:14,870
I can just factor
this out, and I

177
00:18:14,870 --> 00:18:21,600
can write it as 1 plus f
prime of x0 norm, like this.

178
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:22,700
That's fine.

179
00:18:22,700 --> 00:18:25,760
And so now if I want
this thing here--

180
00:18:25,760 --> 00:18:32,530
so now you want to prove
that f is continuous at x0.

181
00:18:32,530 --> 00:18:36,630
So you want this here to be
less than some given epsilon.

182
00:18:36,630 --> 00:18:46,050
So given epsilon
greater than 0, you

183
00:18:46,050 --> 00:18:54,910
let delta 1 be the minimum of--

184
00:18:54,910 --> 00:18:59,730
if you set delta 1 to be
the minimum of the delta

185
00:18:59,730 --> 00:19:04,080
that I already chose, so
then I have this inequality,

186
00:19:04,080 --> 00:19:17,130
and then epsilon over
1 plus f prime of x0.

187
00:19:17,130 --> 00:19:28,430
So if I choose delta like this,
then I have this inequality.

188
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:30,630
I fill in that.

189
00:19:30,630 --> 00:19:38,420
So now I'm assuming that this
thing here is less than delta 1.

190
00:19:38,420 --> 00:19:42,480
So again, I have this inequality
because it's less than delta.

191
00:19:42,480 --> 00:19:49,160
So then when I fill in
here delta 1 for this,

192
00:19:49,160 --> 00:19:56,360
then I see that this thing here
times this is less than epsilon.

193
00:19:56,360 --> 00:20:09,060
You see this holds when x
minus x0 is less than delta 1.

194
00:20:09,060 --> 00:20:13,650
Again, because if it's less than
delta 1, it's less than delta,

195
00:20:13,650 --> 00:20:15,510
so I have this inequality.

196
00:20:15,510 --> 00:20:21,980
And then I can use that delta
1 since this is less than this.

197
00:20:21,980 --> 00:20:24,310
And then I get this inequality.

198
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:31,840
So this is really
what we want to prove.

199
00:20:31,840 --> 00:20:34,240
We want to prove that
if it's differentiable,

200
00:20:34,240 --> 00:20:36,310
then it's also continuous.

201
00:20:36,310 --> 00:20:39,450
And the continuity
here, I proved it

202
00:20:39,450 --> 00:20:44,590
with epsilon and delta that
really should prove it.

203
00:20:48,090 --> 00:20:50,350
Now let's look at some examples.

204
00:20:50,350 --> 00:20:55,600
So this means that if a
function is differentiable,

205
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:56,906
then it's continuous.

206
00:21:01,830 --> 00:21:07,300
Let me give you an example
of a function that is --

207
00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:12,690
so let me give you
three examples.

208
00:21:12,690 --> 00:21:18,010
The first example is
maybe the simplest one.

209
00:21:18,010 --> 00:21:21,030
So this is the
function f of x that

210
00:21:21,030 --> 00:21:24,470
is equal to the
absolute value of x.

211
00:21:24,470 --> 00:21:30,970
So f here is
defined on R into R.

212
00:21:30,970 --> 00:21:33,700
And it's just the
absolute value.

213
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:39,140
And so the absolute value
here we've seen before.

214
00:21:39,140 --> 00:21:42,116
So f here is continuous.

215
00:21:46,630 --> 00:21:52,180
It's continuous at all points,
in particular, of course, at 0.

216
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:55,340
But if you now form the
difference quotient.

217
00:21:55,340 --> 00:21:58,880
Suppose that x here
is bigger than 0.

218
00:21:58,880 --> 00:22:01,700
And now look at the
difference quotient.

219
00:22:01,700 --> 00:22:03,250
So the difference quotient--

220
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:18,400
quotient at x0 equal
to 0 if you're looking

221
00:22:18,400 --> 00:22:21,680
at that difference quotient.

222
00:22:21,680 --> 00:22:24,330
And you first look at
where x is positive.

223
00:22:24,330 --> 00:22:30,710
If x is positive, then
f of x minus f of 0--

224
00:22:30,710 --> 00:22:33,440
x minus 0, this
difference quotient here.

225
00:22:33,440 --> 00:22:37,550
If x is positive, then
this thing here is just x.

226
00:22:37,550 --> 00:22:41,940
So this is x minus 0, x minus 0.

227
00:22:41,940 --> 00:22:44,760
So this here is 1.

228
00:22:44,760 --> 00:22:46,530
This difference quotient is 1.

229
00:22:46,530 --> 00:22:48,780
This is when x is positive.

230
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:55,250
When x is negative,
then f of x--

231
00:22:55,250 --> 00:23:01,730
if x is negative, then the
absolute value is minus x.

232
00:23:01,730 --> 00:23:07,820
So the difference quotient
here f of x minus f of 0,

233
00:23:07,820 --> 00:23:11,150
which is 0, x minus 0--

234
00:23:11,150 --> 00:23:17,960
this here is just
minus x divided by x.

235
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:20,910
But this thing here is minus 1.

236
00:23:20,910 --> 00:23:29,760
So if you're looking at the
difference quotient that

237
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:33,180
comes from looking at
the derivative is 0,

238
00:23:33,180 --> 00:23:37,920
then when you approach
0 from the right,

239
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:40,930
then the difference
quotient is 1.

240
00:23:40,930 --> 00:23:44,740
When you approach it from the--

241
00:23:44,740 --> 00:23:46,740
and when you approach
it from the left,

242
00:23:46,740 --> 00:23:49,360
then the difference
quotient is minus 1.

243
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:51,670
So it doesn't have a limit.

244
00:23:51,670 --> 00:23:54,250
It has a limit from the
right from the left,

245
00:23:54,250 --> 00:23:57,180
but it doesn't have a
limit if you don't restrict

246
00:23:57,180 --> 00:23:59,100
where it's coming from.

247
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:03,850
So this function here
is not differentiable.

248
00:24:03,850 --> 00:24:05,580
Yeah?

249
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:08,460
AUDIENCE: Can you define
what it means when they have

250
00:24:08,460 --> 00:24:11,090
a function of x [INAUDIBLE]?

251
00:24:22,490 --> 00:24:24,730
PROFESSOR: So we did this
earlier in the class.

252
00:24:28,260 --> 00:24:34,610
So that's the
difference quotient here

253
00:24:34,610 --> 00:24:45,380
that this thing here is
converging to some number,

254
00:24:45,380 --> 00:24:47,330
say a.

255
00:24:47,330 --> 00:24:53,810
This here means that for
all epsilon greater than 0,

256
00:24:53,810 --> 00:25:04,280
there exists a delta greater
than 0 such that if x minus x0

257
00:25:04,280 --> 00:25:10,460
is less than delta and,
again, x is assumed

258
00:25:10,460 --> 00:25:12,800
to be different from x0.

259
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:17,070
If you have that, then
this difference quotient

260
00:25:17,070 --> 00:25:31,260
here minus this a is supposed
to be less than epsilon.

261
00:25:31,260 --> 00:25:32,950
And that's this.

262
00:25:37,620 --> 00:25:40,740
And so you see,
again, in this example

263
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:45,660
that we just looked at, where
in the example that we just

264
00:25:45,660 --> 00:25:46,260
looked at--

265
00:25:49,440 --> 00:25:54,030
so in the example
where f of x was

266
00:25:54,030 --> 00:26:00,330
equal to the absolute value of
x, then if you were graphing it,

267
00:26:00,330 --> 00:26:04,200
then this looks like this.

268
00:26:04,200 --> 00:26:07,590
And we saw that the
difference quotient

269
00:26:07,590 --> 00:26:11,380
when you approach it over here,
the difference quotient was 1.

270
00:26:11,380 --> 00:26:14,410
When you approach it over
here, the difference quotient

271
00:26:14,410 --> 00:26:15,620
is minus 1.

272
00:26:15,620 --> 00:26:21,100
So if you take a tiny
little interval around 0,

273
00:26:21,100 --> 00:26:27,740
then the difference quotient
could be either minus 1 or 1.

274
00:26:27,740 --> 00:26:31,550
But however small you
make this little interval,

275
00:26:31,550 --> 00:26:36,050
it's not like that it converges
to some number because it has--

276
00:26:36,050 --> 00:26:37,580
it is both.

277
00:26:37,580 --> 00:26:42,070
There are both cases where it's
1 and cases where it's minus 1.

278
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:48,280
So that's like
the first example.

279
00:26:48,280 --> 00:26:52,840
And again, you know of a
function that is continuous,

280
00:26:52,840 --> 00:26:56,630
but it's not
differentiable at 0.

281
00:26:56,630 --> 00:27:00,460
In this case, it was a 0.

282
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:03,170
And maybe a little bit
more interesting example,

283
00:27:03,170 --> 00:27:08,120
along the same line,
is the following.

284
00:27:30,920 --> 00:27:35,960
So this is, again, a
function on R into R,

285
00:27:35,960 --> 00:27:42,240
and I set f of x equal to
x times sine of 1 over x.

286
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:45,510
This here is not
really defined at 0.

287
00:27:45,510 --> 00:27:47,850
So I need to define
it also at 0.

288
00:27:47,850 --> 00:27:54,540
So when x is equal to 0, then
I just set it equal to 0.

289
00:27:54,540 --> 00:28:02,840
And otherwise, I set it equal
to x times sine of 1 over x.

290
00:28:02,840 --> 00:28:06,260
And so the thing is that this
function here, so f here,

291
00:28:06,260 --> 00:28:07,476
is continuous.

292
00:28:12,540 --> 00:28:18,160
In particular, it's
continuous at 0.

293
00:28:18,160 --> 00:28:19,750
It's actually
continuous everywhere,

294
00:28:19,750 --> 00:28:23,830
but I'm mostly
interested in it at 0.

295
00:28:23,830 --> 00:28:25,480
The graph of this--

296
00:28:25,480 --> 00:28:35,730
so remember that sine function
looks like this, That at 0,

297
00:28:35,730 --> 00:28:41,860
at pi, at 2 pi, at
3 pi, and so on.

298
00:28:41,860 --> 00:28:45,180
And all of these--

299
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:49,870
or at all integer multiples,
also, for the negative ones,

300
00:28:49,870 --> 00:28:56,910
at all integer multiple of
pi, sine function vanishes.

301
00:28:56,910 --> 00:29:01,110
And in between here,
it is at pi over 2,

302
00:29:01,110 --> 00:29:04,540
it achieves its
maximum, which is 1.

303
00:29:04,540 --> 00:29:08,820
And so the function
looks like this.

304
00:29:08,820 --> 00:29:09,840
So this here is 1.

305
00:29:13,690 --> 00:29:17,230
That's how a sine
function looks like.

306
00:29:17,230 --> 00:29:20,320
And so now if you're
looking at sine of 1

307
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:25,990
over x, that's going to be a
function as you're going down.

308
00:29:25,990 --> 00:29:28,990
So if you're looking at
this-- here's a sine of x.

309
00:29:28,990 --> 00:29:34,060
But if you're looking at sine
of 1 over x, as x goes to 0,

310
00:29:34,060 --> 00:29:36,620
this becomes very large.

311
00:29:36,620 --> 00:29:39,490
So this means that
as you're going to 0,

312
00:29:39,490 --> 00:29:45,790
it goes very rapidly
through these periods, where

313
00:29:45,790 --> 00:29:48,620
it oscillates between
negative 1 and 1.

314
00:29:48,620 --> 00:29:50,790
So the value here is minus 1.

315
00:29:53,320 --> 00:30:04,910
And so it will look like this
that it will again fluctuate.

316
00:30:04,910 --> 00:30:10,390
It will fluctuate, but it will
fluctuate much more rapidly.

317
00:30:10,390 --> 00:30:14,173
So if this here,
say, was 1 over pi.

318
00:30:17,370 --> 00:30:24,480
And 1 over pi is the same
as pi, there, it would be 0.

319
00:30:24,480 --> 00:30:26,746
And it would like fluctuate.

320
00:30:30,490 --> 00:30:32,350
So it will look like
something like this,

321
00:30:32,350 --> 00:30:33,700
but it will fluctuate.

322
00:30:36,330 --> 00:30:43,350
And then, as you're going away,
it will fluctuate less often.

323
00:30:43,350 --> 00:30:50,490
So as you're going down towards
0, it will fluctuate a lot.

324
00:30:50,490 --> 00:30:54,520
Because sine always is
between minus 1 and 1,

325
00:30:54,520 --> 00:30:58,660
the value here will always
be between minus 1 and 1.

326
00:30:58,660 --> 00:31:04,300
But as you're going towards 0
from the right or from the left,

327
00:31:04,300 --> 00:31:07,520
it's going to fluctuate
more and more.

328
00:31:07,520 --> 00:31:10,915
As you're going away from 0,
it's fluctuating less and less.

329
00:31:14,600 --> 00:31:17,640
So this is the function
I'm interested in.

330
00:31:17,640 --> 00:31:27,950
But when you multiply
through by x, then you see,

331
00:31:27,950 --> 00:31:31,040
when you multiply through
by x, it's fluctuating,

332
00:31:31,040 --> 00:31:34,400
but it's always fluctuating
between minus 1 and 1.

333
00:31:34,400 --> 00:31:41,180
If you multiply it by x,
then as you're going to 0,

334
00:31:41,180 --> 00:31:48,230
this here will squeeze down, and
the fluctuation will be 0 at 0.

335
00:31:48,230 --> 00:31:52,580
So it's easy to see that this
function here-- so is indeed

336
00:31:52,580 --> 00:31:55,070
continuous.

337
00:31:55,070 --> 00:31:57,390
But let's look at the
difference quotient.

338
00:31:57,390 --> 00:31:59,160
So the difference quotient--

339
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:12,080
so if we look at the difference
quotient, so we're looking at--

340
00:32:12,080 --> 00:32:15,170
and we just look at the
difference quotient at 0.

341
00:32:15,170 --> 00:32:19,490
So this is the
difference quotient.

342
00:32:19,490 --> 00:32:23,630
And so it's x sine 1 over x.

343
00:32:23,630 --> 00:32:26,605
The value at 0 by
definition was 0.

344
00:32:30,380 --> 00:32:34,250
And so you see
that now this here,

345
00:32:34,250 --> 00:32:39,980
this here becomes just x
sine 1 over x divided by x.

346
00:32:39,980 --> 00:32:42,690
And so you see that this
here cancel with that.

347
00:32:42,690 --> 00:32:45,950
So you just get sine 1 over x.

348
00:32:45,950 --> 00:32:49,020
And so you see that
now, as x goes to--

349
00:32:49,020 --> 00:32:53,870
so when x here goes to
0, this here fluctuates.

350
00:32:53,870 --> 00:33:07,620
This here fluctuates
between minus 1 and 1.

351
00:33:07,620 --> 00:33:10,020
But it doesn't have
a limit because it's

352
00:33:10,020 --> 00:33:11,580
fluctuating wider and wider.

353
00:33:11,580 --> 00:33:16,120
So this is an example again--

354
00:33:16,120 --> 00:33:21,780
this is another example of a
function that is continuous at 0

355
00:33:21,780 --> 00:33:23,320
but not differentiable at 0.

356
00:33:23,320 --> 00:33:25,145
It's actually much
worse than the previous.

357
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:37,290
The previous example that was--
this example is much more tame.

358
00:33:37,290 --> 00:33:39,790
It is not differentiable at 0.

359
00:33:39,790 --> 00:33:44,460
But if you were to restrict
to just positive things,

360
00:33:44,460 --> 00:33:47,380
then it would be differentiable.

361
00:33:47,380 --> 00:33:50,160
You can also define
derivative-- so,

362
00:33:50,160 --> 00:33:53,020
in general, when we
define derivative,

363
00:33:53,020 --> 00:33:56,410
we let it be defined in a
neighborhood of that point,

364
00:33:56,410 --> 00:33:57,970
so on either side.

365
00:33:57,970 --> 00:34:00,280
But if you were
considering where you were,

366
00:34:00,280 --> 00:34:04,450
allowing the function just
to be defined at this point

367
00:34:04,450 --> 00:34:07,630
and then to the right,
say, or to the left,

368
00:34:07,630 --> 00:34:10,690
then this function
would be differentiable.

369
00:34:10,690 --> 00:34:15,639
But in this example
here, it doesn't

370
00:34:15,639 --> 00:34:19,060
matter if you restrict the
interval to just to the right

371
00:34:19,060 --> 00:34:19,850
or to the left.

372
00:34:19,850 --> 00:34:21,280
It's still not differentiable.

373
00:34:21,280 --> 00:34:26,980
So this says something about
this example, something

374
00:34:26,980 --> 00:34:32,230
about that it fluctuates
wildly and that is causing it

375
00:34:32,230 --> 00:34:35,920
not to be differentiable.

376
00:34:35,920 --> 00:34:40,100
Again, if you defined it--

377
00:34:40,100 --> 00:34:43,570
if you didn't have this
kind of-- the x here

378
00:34:43,570 --> 00:34:47,080
is serving as some
of-- it's dampening

379
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:49,040
how much it's fluctuating.

380
00:34:49,040 --> 00:34:54,340
So if there wasn't an x here,
then this function wouldn't-- it

381
00:34:54,340 --> 00:34:58,090
would just fluctuate, and it
wouldn't even be continuous

382
00:34:58,090 --> 00:34:59,400
at x0.

383
00:34:59,400 --> 00:35:03,360
But once I put this x on,
then it becomes continuous.

384
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:07,890
If I put another x,
so if I'm looking

385
00:35:07,890 --> 00:35:16,528
at f of x equal to x
squared times sine 1 over x.

386
00:35:16,528 --> 00:35:18,070
Suppose I'm looking
at this function.

387
00:35:18,070 --> 00:35:23,260
So now I have dampened
the fluctuation even more.

388
00:35:23,260 --> 00:35:26,670
And so if I came looking at
the difference quotient--

389
00:35:26,670 --> 00:35:29,730
so I'm looking at this function.

390
00:35:29,730 --> 00:35:31,210
I should be more careful.

391
00:35:31,210 --> 00:35:36,610
So again, at 0, I need
to define it separately.

392
00:35:36,610 --> 00:35:43,270
So I define a function
that is 0 at x equal to 0.

393
00:35:43,270 --> 00:35:49,880
And then it is x squared
times sine 1 over x otherwise.

394
00:35:53,980 --> 00:35:56,010
If I'm looking at
this function, then I

395
00:35:56,010 --> 00:36:03,490
claim that this function is, in
fact, differentiable at x at 0.

396
00:36:03,490 --> 00:36:06,850
And so let's look at that.

397
00:36:06,850 --> 00:36:11,350
So again, I'm looking at
the difference quotient.

398
00:36:11,350 --> 00:36:14,090
And the value at 0 is 0.

399
00:36:14,090 --> 00:36:15,195
So I have this.

400
00:36:18,130 --> 00:36:25,060
And so this here is now just
x squared sine 1 over x.

401
00:36:25,060 --> 00:36:27,020
This thing here is then x.

402
00:36:27,020 --> 00:36:30,700
And so you see that this
difference quotient is just

403
00:36:30,700 --> 00:36:31,720
this.

404
00:36:31,720 --> 00:36:38,630
But this here does converge
to 0 as x goes to 0.

405
00:36:38,630 --> 00:36:41,650
So this function is
actually differentiable.

406
00:36:41,650 --> 00:36:48,100
It's fluctuates, but it's more
mild the way it fluctuates.

407
00:36:48,100 --> 00:36:54,140
Now let me get
back to the rules.

408
00:36:54,140 --> 00:36:59,410
So we proved one rule
that was this sum rule.

409
00:37:03,080 --> 00:37:05,480
Then the second rule
we're interested in

410
00:37:05,480 --> 00:37:08,070
is when we're looking
at the Leibniz rule.

411
00:37:08,070 --> 00:37:11,180
So we're looking at the
product of these two functions.

412
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:17,510
And each function is assumed
to be differentiable at x0.

413
00:37:17,510 --> 00:37:22,100
And then we claim that the
product function is also

414
00:37:22,100 --> 00:37:24,240
differentiable at x0.

415
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:28,700
And this is the formula
for the derivative.

416
00:37:28,700 --> 00:37:32,750
To prove this one here,
I need these facts

417
00:37:32,750 --> 00:37:36,480
that we have now proved that
if it is differentiable,

418
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:39,380
then it's also
continuous at that point.

419
00:37:39,380 --> 00:37:41,695
So I'm going to use that fact.

420
00:37:51,410 --> 00:38:01,790
So this is proof
of Leibniz rule.

421
00:38:08,010 --> 00:38:11,050
So we have these two functions.

422
00:38:11,050 --> 00:38:13,537
We're looking at f of x.

423
00:38:13,537 --> 00:38:14,870
We form the difference quotient.

424
00:38:22,230 --> 00:38:25,685
So this is the difference
quotient, x minus x0.

425
00:38:29,580 --> 00:38:34,170
We do this trick that we've
done a bunch of times before,

426
00:38:34,170 --> 00:38:39,090
when we also looked at products
if we had two sequences,

427
00:38:39,090 --> 00:38:41,430
and we looked at
the sequence that

428
00:38:41,430 --> 00:38:43,720
was the product of these two.

429
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:48,510
So just a reminder that--

430
00:38:48,510 --> 00:38:49,660
I mean, it's not like--

431
00:38:59,440 --> 00:39:04,340
so remember that if you had a
sequence an and a sequence bn,

432
00:39:04,340 --> 00:39:08,890
and you knew that an converges
to a and bn converges

433
00:39:08,890 --> 00:39:18,520
to b, then the product here
converges to a times b.

434
00:39:18,520 --> 00:39:23,320
And when we prove that,
we use this little trick

435
00:39:23,320 --> 00:39:29,320
that we're splitting the
difference between this

436
00:39:29,320 --> 00:39:30,830
and this.

437
00:39:30,830 --> 00:39:34,690
And so I'm going to do
exactly the same trick here.

438
00:39:34,690 --> 00:39:37,070
So I'm looking at
this thing here.

439
00:39:37,070 --> 00:39:43,940
And so I write this thing here
as f of x times g of x minus--

440
00:39:43,940 --> 00:39:48,410
and it doesn't really matter
where you're inserting it.

441
00:39:48,410 --> 00:39:51,450
So this here is
splitting the difference.

442
00:39:51,450 --> 00:39:53,020
And then I have to add it again.

443
00:40:02,180 --> 00:40:06,590
So this is certainly the
same, just subtracting it

444
00:40:06,590 --> 00:40:10,100
and adding it right back.

445
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:13,170
And now I split this into two.

446
00:40:13,170 --> 00:40:16,110
So I write this thing here.

447
00:40:16,110 --> 00:40:20,150
So this fraction,
I split into two.

448
00:40:20,150 --> 00:40:23,880
And the first one, I take
this one here together.

449
00:40:23,880 --> 00:40:27,060
And you can factor out a g of x.

450
00:40:27,060 --> 00:40:36,080
You can write it like this, f
of x minus f of x0, x minus x0.

451
00:40:36,080 --> 00:40:39,290
So I'm just here.

452
00:40:39,290 --> 00:40:44,540
I'm factoring out the g of x,
and then I'm left with this.

453
00:40:44,540 --> 00:40:47,600
And this is keeping the
denominator, like that.

454
00:40:47,600 --> 00:40:49,930
This is this part here.

455
00:40:49,930 --> 00:40:52,480
And then I have the
other part here.

456
00:40:52,480 --> 00:40:56,235
But there, you see that the
f of x0 is factoring out.

457
00:40:58,780 --> 00:41:10,180
And then I have g of x minus g
of x0, x minus x0, like that.

458
00:41:10,180 --> 00:41:17,170
But now you see that
as x goes to x0, well,

459
00:41:17,170 --> 00:41:20,830
g was continuous at x0.

460
00:41:20,830 --> 00:41:25,400
So this thing here,
as x goes to x0,

461
00:41:25,400 --> 00:41:29,930
this thing here
goes to g of x0--

462
00:41:29,930 --> 00:41:30,620
this one here.

463
00:41:30,620 --> 00:41:32,810
That was because
g was continuous.

464
00:41:32,810 --> 00:41:35,930
Because it was differentiable,
so it was also continuous.

465
00:41:35,930 --> 00:41:38,510
This here goes to
the derivative.

466
00:41:41,350 --> 00:41:47,780
And the second thing here
this is just constant.

467
00:41:47,780 --> 00:41:55,260
And this goes to the
derivative, like that.

468
00:41:55,260 --> 00:42:01,040
So what we have proven this
here is as x goes to x0.

469
00:42:01,040 --> 00:42:04,400
So you see that we have
proven that this difference

470
00:42:04,400 --> 00:42:09,350
quotient here for the
product has a limit.

471
00:42:09,350 --> 00:42:11,970
And this is what the limit is.

472
00:42:11,970 --> 00:42:15,200
And so this means that the
product is differentiable.

473
00:42:15,200 --> 00:42:17,820
And this here is the derivative.

474
00:42:17,820 --> 00:42:21,180
And that's exactly
the Leibniz formula,

475
00:42:21,180 --> 00:42:24,510
which was this over
here, this formula here.

476
00:42:27,320 --> 00:42:34,490
The next thing is that we
want to prove the next rule is

477
00:42:34,490 --> 00:42:37,370
like this quotient rule.

478
00:42:37,370 --> 00:42:40,165
So let me prove
the quotient rule.

479
00:42:58,320 --> 00:43:04,680
So Leibniz and Newton
was the one that

480
00:43:04,680 --> 00:43:09,993
was responsible for calculus.

481
00:43:13,770 --> 00:43:16,110
And so then the next
thing I want to prove

482
00:43:16,110 --> 00:43:17,950
is this quotient rule.

483
00:43:27,870 --> 00:43:31,535
And so, again, you have
these two functions, f and g.

484
00:43:36,360 --> 00:43:49,190
And they are
differentiable at x0.

485
00:43:49,190 --> 00:43:58,430
And I'm also assuming that g
here at x0 is not equal to x.

486
00:43:58,430 --> 00:44:02,800
Let's just assume that g is
not equal to 0 everywhere.

487
00:44:02,800 --> 00:44:08,420
It's different
from 0 everywhere.

488
00:44:13,870 --> 00:44:18,280
And then the quotient
rule says that if you're

489
00:44:18,280 --> 00:44:25,210
looking at the quotient here and
you take the derivative of the--

490
00:44:25,210 --> 00:44:29,320
so it say that if you're
looking at the quotient here--

491
00:44:29,320 --> 00:44:33,910
so the quotient will say that
if you're looking at this thing

492
00:44:33,910 --> 00:44:37,700
here, this now makes sense
because g does not vanish.

493
00:44:37,700 --> 00:44:48,470
This here is
differentiable at x0.

494
00:44:48,470 --> 00:44:56,480
And the derivative of
this function at x0

495
00:44:56,480 --> 00:44:59,270
is given by the
following formula,

496
00:44:59,270 --> 00:45:07,700
that it is the derivative
of f at x0 times g of x0

497
00:45:07,700 --> 00:45:20,100
minus f at x0 times g prime
at x0 divided by g squared.

498
00:45:20,100 --> 00:45:23,460
So g of x minus 0 times itself--

499
00:45:23,460 --> 00:45:24,965
I'll write that as a square.

500
00:45:28,370 --> 00:45:30,530
Write this here
again-- this here,

501
00:45:30,530 --> 00:45:35,750
this is just g of
x0 times g of x0.

502
00:45:40,360 --> 00:45:44,475
So now let's try to prove that.

503
00:46:09,550 --> 00:46:14,380
So we're looking at
f of x like this.

504
00:46:14,380 --> 00:46:19,540
At x minus-- so this is
the difference version--

505
00:46:19,540 --> 00:46:24,920
at x0 divided by x minus x0.

506
00:46:24,920 --> 00:46:26,530
That's the difference equation.

507
00:46:26,530 --> 00:46:40,490
And so written out, this is
f of x g of x minus f of x0

508
00:46:40,490 --> 00:46:46,580
divided by g of x0 x minus x0.

509
00:46:46,580 --> 00:46:52,110
So let me put this on a fraction
with a common denominator.

510
00:46:52,110 --> 00:46:56,730
So if I do that, then
this thing here is--

511
00:46:56,730 --> 00:47:01,940
so then I'm
multiplying by g of x0

512
00:47:01,940 --> 00:47:05,690
both in the numerator
and the denominator here.

513
00:47:05,690 --> 00:47:12,350
So I'm getting f
of x times g of x0.

514
00:47:12,350 --> 00:47:14,690
And then here, I'm
multiplying by g

515
00:47:14,690 --> 00:47:18,470
of x in both numerator and
denominator, so getting

516
00:47:18,470 --> 00:47:22,670
this thing here, g of x.

517
00:47:22,670 --> 00:47:33,230
And then it becomes x minus
x0 and then g of x, g of x0,

518
00:47:33,230 --> 00:47:33,775
like that.

519
00:47:37,260 --> 00:47:43,900
And so now you can write
this thing here as--

520
00:47:43,900 --> 00:47:49,770
and so now you do the
same trick as before.

521
00:47:49,770 --> 00:47:54,540
I mean, I could have gotten
a way slightly easier

522
00:47:54,540 --> 00:47:59,730
with looking at a special
case of this quotient rule,

523
00:47:59,730 --> 00:48:02,260
where f was a
constant function 1.

524
00:48:02,260 --> 00:48:04,480
So I could do that first.

525
00:48:04,480 --> 00:48:07,890
And then the general
case, I could then from--

526
00:48:07,890 --> 00:48:13,750
get from this special case
together with Leibniz rule.

527
00:48:13,750 --> 00:48:16,420
It's most often I want
to do it together anyway,

528
00:48:16,420 --> 00:48:18,400
but if you have
to do it together,

529
00:48:18,400 --> 00:48:22,050
you have to use the same
trick that we just did.

530
00:48:22,050 --> 00:48:39,280
So we're using that over
here, so f of g of x0 minus

531
00:48:39,280 --> 00:48:57,520
and then f of x0, g of
x0, Plus f of x0 g of x0

532
00:48:57,520 --> 00:49:03,170
minus f of x0 g of
x-- so this is, again,

533
00:49:03,170 --> 00:49:05,900
where I'm subtracting in the
denominator or something,

534
00:49:05,900 --> 00:49:09,220
and then I'm adding it
back up immediately.

535
00:49:09,220 --> 00:49:19,260
So I have this, like that.

536
00:49:21,940 --> 00:49:27,800
And so now if you do
that, then you see that--

537
00:49:27,800 --> 00:49:33,080
and now you split it up,
again, into two fractions.

538
00:49:33,080 --> 00:49:35,630
So we split it up.

539
00:49:35,630 --> 00:49:40,490
And so this thing here,
this thing here, but there,

540
00:49:40,490 --> 00:49:43,590
you see that there's a g
of x here that factor out.

541
00:49:43,590 --> 00:49:47,420
So this is equal to g of x0.

542
00:49:47,420 --> 00:49:54,080
And then you have f of
x minus f of x0 and then

543
00:49:54,080 --> 00:50:03,680
x minus x0, g of x,
g of x0, like that.

544
00:50:03,680 --> 00:50:09,020
And for the second one, you
see that there, you also

545
00:50:09,020 --> 00:50:11,120
have something that factor out.

546
00:50:11,120 --> 00:50:17,420
And this is f of x0, like that.

547
00:50:17,420 --> 00:50:23,840
And you're getting
g of x0 minus g

548
00:50:23,840 --> 00:50:37,840
of x over x minus x0, g
of x, g of x0, like that.

549
00:50:37,840 --> 00:50:46,030
And so now I can make
this looks nicer.

550
00:50:46,030 --> 00:50:50,440
I can, this one here,
cancel with that.

551
00:50:50,440 --> 00:50:53,050
And so I can write
this thing here

552
00:50:53,050 --> 00:51:03,820
as 1 over g of x f of x minus
f of x0 divided by x minus x0.

553
00:51:03,820 --> 00:51:06,150
So let's take care of this here.

554
00:51:08,710 --> 00:51:16,100
Here, I will write it as I
will take this one here out.

555
00:51:16,100 --> 00:51:26,200
So I'll write it as f of
x0 of x times g of x0.

556
00:51:26,200 --> 00:51:29,090
And then I'm left
with this thing here.

557
00:51:29,090 --> 00:51:31,430
And what I don't love
about this thing here

558
00:51:31,430 --> 00:51:34,340
is it's not quite the
difference quotient

559
00:51:34,340 --> 00:51:37,070
because the order of
these two is wrong.

560
00:51:37,070 --> 00:51:39,930
So I switched the order.

561
00:51:39,930 --> 00:51:46,170
But if I switch the order, then
I have to pick up a minus sign.

562
00:51:46,170 --> 00:51:47,880
So I get minus here.

563
00:51:47,880 --> 00:51:58,650
And now it is g of x, g of
x0, x minus x0, like that.

564
00:51:58,650 --> 00:52:05,150
And now you see that
as x goes to x0,

565
00:52:05,150 --> 00:52:12,620
so now as x goes to x0, well,
this one here, the function

566
00:52:12,620 --> 00:52:13,530
was differentiable.

567
00:52:13,530 --> 00:52:14,730
So it's also continuous.

568
00:52:14,730 --> 00:52:18,740
So this goes to 1 over g of x0.

569
00:52:18,740 --> 00:52:25,640
This one here just goes
to the derivative at x0.

570
00:52:25,640 --> 00:52:29,010
This one here goes to--
again, because the function

571
00:52:29,010 --> 00:52:33,540
is continuous, this
goes to minus f of x0.

572
00:52:33,540 --> 00:52:36,495
And then it's what I
wrote before as g squared.

573
00:52:36,495 --> 00:52:40,920
It's g of x0 times itself.

574
00:52:40,920 --> 00:52:47,910
And then this thing here goes
to the derivative of x0 at 0.

575
00:52:47,910 --> 00:52:53,410
And now if I want to, I can
rewrite this thing here.

576
00:52:53,410 --> 00:52:56,470
If I want to put it back
on the common denominator,

577
00:52:56,470 --> 00:53:00,570
then I have to multiply in
the numerator and denominator

578
00:53:00,570 --> 00:53:02,320
by g of x0.

579
00:53:02,320 --> 00:53:10,170
If I do this, I get f
prime of x0, g of x0,

580
00:53:10,170 --> 00:53:15,640
and then I get g squared
of x0 in the denominator.

581
00:53:15,640 --> 00:53:25,170
And then here it's just those
two minus f of x0 g prime of x0.

582
00:53:25,170 --> 00:53:29,440
And so you see that this
here is exactly this formula

583
00:53:29,440 --> 00:53:31,540
here, this quotient rule.

584
00:53:31,540 --> 00:53:38,290
So this proof, that if you
take two functions, the one

585
00:53:38,290 --> 00:53:41,270
you have in the denominator,
if that is nonvanishing,

586
00:53:41,270 --> 00:53:43,600
So You can form the quotient.

587
00:53:43,600 --> 00:53:47,300
And if each of these two
functions is differentiable,

588
00:53:47,300 --> 00:53:50,150
then the quotient is
also differentiable.

589
00:53:50,150 --> 00:53:53,600
And this is the formula
for the derivative.

590
00:53:53,600 --> 00:53:57,250
So now why is this already
really, really useful?

591
00:53:57,250 --> 00:54:11,440
So we calculated-- so we started
with the example of where

592
00:54:11,440 --> 00:54:13,880
f is a constant.

593
00:54:13,880 --> 00:54:17,050
And we saw that
it's differentiable,

594
00:54:17,050 --> 00:54:19,060
and the derivative is 0.

595
00:54:19,060 --> 00:54:24,740
Then the second example we had
was where the function was x,

596
00:54:24,740 --> 00:54:26,810
and we saw that it
was differentiable

597
00:54:26,810 --> 00:54:29,580
and the derivative is 1.

598
00:54:29,580 --> 00:54:32,160
If you now take--

599
00:54:32,160 --> 00:54:36,570
if you now take ax times--

600
00:54:36,570 --> 00:54:39,860
so let's just take ax
as the next example.

601
00:54:39,860 --> 00:54:45,300
This is like a constant
function times this function.

602
00:54:45,300 --> 00:54:48,140
So Leibniz rule--
so if f of x is

603
00:54:48,140 --> 00:54:51,750
equal to a times x,
where A is a constant,

604
00:54:51,750 --> 00:54:55,040
Leibniz will say that the
product of two differentiable

605
00:54:55,040 --> 00:54:59,480
function is differentiable,
and the derivative of this

606
00:54:59,480 --> 00:55:01,398
is the derivative of that.

607
00:55:01,398 --> 00:55:03,690
But the derivative of that,
that's a constant function.

608
00:55:03,690 --> 00:55:04,380
So that's 0.

609
00:55:04,380 --> 00:55:06,300
So I don't even have
to think about that.

610
00:55:06,300 --> 00:55:09,710
So it's just the a
times the derivative

611
00:55:09,710 --> 00:55:10,710
of the second function.

612
00:55:10,710 --> 00:55:11,460
But that's one.

613
00:55:11,460 --> 00:55:15,660
So this here is the
derivative of that.

614
00:55:15,660 --> 00:55:21,440
And the next example would be
that you're looking at ax plus,

615
00:55:21,440 --> 00:55:22,765
say, b.

616
00:55:22,765 --> 00:55:25,050
f of x is equal to this.

617
00:55:25,050 --> 00:55:28,450
Then you can use the sum plus--

618
00:55:28,450 --> 00:55:31,030
we know what each of these is.

619
00:55:31,030 --> 00:55:34,300
So you get that this one
here is also differentiable.

620
00:55:34,300 --> 00:55:38,790
And the derivative of this
function here is also just a.

621
00:55:38,790 --> 00:55:43,150
And you can continue like
this, that if you take--

622
00:55:43,150 --> 00:55:47,500
and if you now
have a polynomial,

623
00:55:47,500 --> 00:55:50,640
suppose you take
a to the power n.

624
00:55:50,640 --> 00:55:55,950
So if f of x is
x to the power n,

625
00:55:55,950 --> 00:55:58,140
then you can think
about this thing

626
00:55:58,140 --> 00:56:03,330
here as x times x
times x times x.

627
00:56:03,330 --> 00:56:05,800
And you can use
the Leibniz rule.

628
00:56:05,800 --> 00:56:08,770
And so if you use
the Leibniz rule,

629
00:56:08,770 --> 00:56:12,720
so you just do it first
in the case where f of x

630
00:56:12,720 --> 00:56:16,660
is equal to x squared, you can
think about this as x times x.

631
00:56:16,660 --> 00:56:18,970
These are two
differentiable functions.

632
00:56:18,970 --> 00:56:23,190
So the derivative here is
now the derivative of this.

633
00:56:23,190 --> 00:56:26,130
So that's 1 times
the other function.

634
00:56:26,130 --> 00:56:30,480
Plus you keep this one here and
take the derivative of that.

635
00:56:30,480 --> 00:56:31,590
So this is this.

636
00:56:31,590 --> 00:56:35,420
So this is 2 times x.

637
00:56:35,420 --> 00:56:40,520
And so if you take this function
here using Leibniz rule.

638
00:56:40,520 --> 00:56:43,700
In this case, the derivative
is differentiable,

639
00:56:43,700 --> 00:56:50,065
and the derivative is n times
x to the power n minus 1.

640
00:56:55,340 --> 00:56:58,280
And in fact, even
more generally,

641
00:56:58,280 --> 00:57:05,300
just using the sum
and the Leibniz rule,

642
00:57:05,300 --> 00:57:13,220
if you take any polynomial
an xn plus an minus 1 xn

643
00:57:13,220 --> 00:57:20,130
minus 1 down to the last thing
is being a constant-- if you

644
00:57:20,130 --> 00:57:24,000
take a polynomial like
this just using the Leibniz

645
00:57:24,000 --> 00:57:29,190
rule and the sum rule,
using those repeatedly,

646
00:57:29,190 --> 00:57:32,190
we know that this
is differentiable

647
00:57:32,190 --> 00:57:34,890
and the derivative
is just like the sum

648
00:57:34,890 --> 00:57:36,190
of the derivative of these.

649
00:57:36,190 --> 00:57:42,900
So this is going to be an times
n times x to the power n minus 1

650
00:57:42,900 --> 00:57:51,420
plus n minus 1 times
n minus 1 xn minus 2.

651
00:57:51,420 --> 00:57:54,550
And then this one here,
the derivative is 0.

652
00:57:54,550 --> 00:57:58,590
So the last one you're getting
something in nontrivial form

653
00:57:58,590 --> 00:58:01,840
is a1.

654
00:58:01,840 --> 00:58:04,590
So this is just using
sum and Leibniz rule.

655
00:58:04,590 --> 00:58:21,190
And so this is just using
the sum plus Leibniz rule

656
00:58:21,190 --> 00:58:21,910
repeatedly.

657
00:58:28,400 --> 00:58:33,430
Now likewise, if you
take a rational function,

658
00:58:33,430 --> 00:58:36,560
so meaning that if
you take something--

659
00:58:36,560 --> 00:58:41,800
if f of x is equal
to some polynomial--

660
00:58:45,760 --> 00:58:54,640
so let's go p of x divided
by q of x, so where this here

661
00:58:54,640 --> 00:59:03,790
is a polynomial and so is that.

662
00:59:03,790 --> 00:59:07,540
So now you can use
the quotient rule.

663
00:59:15,680 --> 00:59:17,180
So the quotient
rule will tell you

664
00:59:17,180 --> 00:59:21,170
that as long as you are looking
at point where this here

665
00:59:21,170 --> 00:59:25,040
does not vanish, so then
the quotient here is also

666
00:59:25,040 --> 00:59:28,250
differentiable, and
it gives you a formula

667
00:59:28,250 --> 00:59:31,010
for the derivative in terms
of the derivative of each

668
00:59:31,010 --> 00:59:31,950
of these two.

669
00:59:31,950 --> 00:59:35,480
And each of those two, they
can be calculated like that.

670
00:59:35,480 --> 00:59:42,200
So you see, already now, we
have quite the derivative

671
00:59:42,200 --> 00:59:43,620
of a lot of functions.

672
00:59:43,620 --> 00:59:47,040
We have of all function and
of all rational functions.

673
00:59:47,040 --> 00:59:51,020
Rational functions, again,
are quotients of a polynomial

674
00:59:51,020 --> 00:59:54,770
by other polynomial.

675
00:59:54,770 --> 01:00:00,680
So now the next rule that
we want to talk about

676
01:00:00,680 --> 01:00:03,340
is the chain rule.

677
01:00:07,220 --> 01:00:09,950
So what is the chain rule?

678
01:00:09,950 --> 01:00:14,550
So the chain rule is a little
bit different from those three

679
01:00:14,550 --> 01:00:16,410
rules here.

680
01:00:16,410 --> 01:00:22,200
And it is where you take the
composition of functions.

681
01:00:22,200 --> 01:00:24,400
So the next is the chain rule.

682
01:00:30,060 --> 01:00:40,900
And again, this is about
composition of functions.

683
01:00:44,340 --> 01:00:51,990
So I have a function f
that goes, say, from--

684
01:00:51,990 --> 01:00:54,180
to say that it goes
from some interval a

685
01:00:54,180 --> 01:01:00,840
to b into another
interval from c to d.

686
01:01:00,840 --> 01:01:06,300
And then I have a function g
that goes from this interval

687
01:01:06,300 --> 01:01:20,450
here into R because g is
defined whatever the image of f

688
01:01:20,450 --> 01:01:25,950
always, if it's in the set,
this is where g is defined.

689
01:01:25,950 --> 01:01:33,350
Then you have that you can form
if you take some x in here--

690
01:01:33,350 --> 01:01:39,450
so if x here is in a to b,
then this here makes sense.

691
01:01:39,450 --> 01:01:43,020
And this is now an element here.

692
01:01:43,020 --> 01:01:45,120
So this is where g is defined.

693
01:01:45,120 --> 01:01:47,360
So this here makes sense.

694
01:01:47,360 --> 01:01:51,060
And this is now what we
call the composition.

695
01:01:51,060 --> 01:01:52,920
And we write it like this.

696
01:01:52,920 --> 01:01:58,220
It's important to write
it in the correct order.

697
01:01:58,220 --> 01:02:00,430
And so this is the
composition function.

698
01:02:08,330 --> 01:02:10,870
So now the chain rule
is the following.

699
01:02:23,730 --> 01:02:25,790
So the chain rule is
a foreign theorem.

700
01:02:36,480 --> 01:02:53,310
Then if f is
differentiable at x0

701
01:02:53,310 --> 01:03:09,010
and g is differentiable
at y0, where

702
01:03:09,010 --> 01:03:17,660
y0 is equal to the value of f
at x0, then the composition--

703
01:03:17,660 --> 01:03:20,650
then the composition
of these two functions

704
01:03:20,650 --> 01:03:25,720
is differentiable at
x0, and the derivative

705
01:03:25,720 --> 01:03:31,270
is equal to the derivative
of g at y0 times

706
01:03:31,270 --> 01:03:34,940
the derivative of f at x0.

707
01:03:34,940 --> 01:03:36,790
So this is the chain rule.

708
01:03:36,790 --> 01:03:39,760
The chain rule says that
the composition is also

709
01:03:39,760 --> 01:03:43,720
differentiable at
x0, and this is

710
01:03:43,720 --> 01:03:47,840
the formula for the derivative.

711
01:03:47,840 --> 01:03:50,230
Let's try to prove that.

712
01:03:50,230 --> 01:03:54,940
And so, in this case,
it's useful to divide up

713
01:03:54,940 --> 01:03:56,883
to two cases.

714
01:04:03,580 --> 01:04:07,520
And the two cases to
divide up to-- so let

715
01:04:07,520 --> 01:04:12,030
me just look at a
special case first,

716
01:04:12,030 --> 01:04:16,940
and then once we understand
that, the general case will

717
01:04:16,940 --> 01:04:17,960
be clear.

718
01:04:17,960 --> 01:04:20,370
So let's make one assumption.

719
01:04:20,370 --> 01:04:21,415
So let's try to prove it.

720
01:04:25,310 --> 01:04:34,736
But we assume first
that the derivative--

721
01:04:37,250 --> 01:04:41,630
I'm going to assume that
the derivative at x0

722
01:04:41,630 --> 01:04:43,410
is not equal to 0.

723
01:04:43,410 --> 01:04:48,350
So I'm assuming that, first,
what does this guarantee me?

724
01:04:48,350 --> 01:04:53,150
Well, remember that the
derivative is the limit

725
01:04:53,150 --> 01:04:54,355
of this difference quotient.

726
01:04:58,220 --> 01:05:01,880
So this is the limit.

727
